# Copy to .env and fill in. The defaults below work with the bundled compose.yaml.

POSTGRES_USER=perolehan
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=perolehan
POSTGRES_DB=perolehan
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

DATABASE_URL="postgresql://perolehan:perolehan@localhost:5432/perolehan?schema=public"

# The running app's own database login (ADR 0034 D11, Phase 1d). `npm run db:roles` (run by db:migrate, db:deploy and setup) makes the
# role APP_DB_ROLE (unset: perolehan_app) with the password APP_DB_PASSWORD, able to read and write every table but never to change or
# delete the audit trail or the sign-in log. Unset: no role is made and the app keeps running as the owner, which development allows
# (the accounts screen says so) and production refuses at sign-in. Unset where the role already exists (a migration job that holds only
# the owner's login): the role's login and password are left alone and only its grants are brought into line, which revokes on any
# trail table the migration just made. To switch a copy over: set APP_DB_PASSWORD to a long random value,
# run `npm run db:roles`, then point DATABASE_URL at the role and MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL at the owner, and restart.
# The role is cluster-wide: every database on the same server shares it and its one password.
# APP_DB_PASSWORD=
# APP_DB_ROLE=perolehan_app
# MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://perolehan:perolehan@localhost:5432/perolehan?schema=public"
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://perolehan_app:<APP_DB_PASSWORD>@localhost:5432/perolehan?schema=public"
# Careful: once MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL is set, pointing DATABASE_URL at another database on the command line no longer moves the Prisma
# CLI or db:roles; set MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL there instead.

# The deployment's one organisation (ADR 0034 D10). ORGANISATION_CODE names the organisation row the public pages, the sign-in
# screens and the loaders act for before anyone signs in (unset: "ORG"); after sign-in the signed-in person's organisation is used.
# ORGANISATION_NAME is the legal name `npm run setup` gives that row when it creates it, and the name the frame shows only while
# there is no organisation row yet. Printed records (certificates, award letters, tender documents) name the organisation from its
# own row, which is edited under Configuration, Organisation, never from this variable. ORGANISATION_STRAPLINE is the line under
# the name in the sidebar (unset: "Procurement"; blank is fine).
# ORGANISATION_CODE=ORG
# ORGANISATION_NAME=Your organisation
# ORGANISATION_STRAPLINE=Procurement

# Which door this copy of the product is the front of. Leave it unset (or "internal") for the
# officers' copy, which serves every screen. A copy that strangers can reach, the supplier portal
# on a public address, must be started with PORTAL_SURFACE=public: it then serves only the portal's
# pages and its one registration POST, and answers everything else, including every staff screen
# and every server action, with 404. There is no sign-in yet, so without this the staff screens
# are open to anyone who can reach the copy. Any value other than empty or "internal" closes the
# door, so a misspelling cannot open it. See src/proxy.ts and src/lib/portal/surface.ts.
# PORTAL_SURFACE=public

# The key that signs the supplier portal's forms (Phase 4a): the registration form and the supplier sign-in, activation and reset forms each carry
# a token signed with it, and the portal refuses a post without a good one. At least 32 characters, long and random, kept out of the repository
# (for example: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(36).toString('base64url'))"). Unset or too short, those forms are closed
# and the registration form says the portal's form key is still to be set. Changing it only retires the forms already open on someone's screen.
# PORTAL_FORM_SECRET=

# Document store (ADR 0011). The driver defaults to postgres, the only one built.
# DOCUMENT_MAX_BYTES is the deployment's technical ceiling on one file, in bytes; with it
# unset every upload is refused. The organisation's own limit is set in the portal under
# Configuration, Documents, Upload rules, and the smaller of the two applies.
#
# Memory: a file is held whole while it is handled, so one request needs a multiple of
# DOCUMENT_MAX_BYTES. An upload holds about 4 times the file (the parsed form part, the
# copy taken from it, and the database driver's own copy when writing; measured at 2 to 3
# times through parsing alone, down from 7 to 8 before the body was parsed once). A
# download holds about 3 times (the driver's result, the decoded bytes, the response;
# an estimate, not measured). Size the host for
#   uploads cap x 4 x ceiling  +  downloads cap x 3 x ceiling  (plus the application's own ~0.5 GB)
# and measure on the real hardware; the 50 MB below with the defaults is about 1.5 GB, before
# the application's own memory. Only the parse was measured: the driver's copy and the
# download figure are estimates.
# The caps count requests in flight in this process only (one per instance: several
# instances multiply them). A download's slot is held while the response is built, not
# while a slow client reads it, so the caps do not bound slow readers. A request over a cap is answered 503 with Retry-After and is
# not an access event. An unset, zero or non-numeric value is the default.
DOCUMENT_STORE_DRIVER=postgres
DOCUMENT_MAX_BYTES=52428800
DOCUMENT_MAX_CONCURRENT_UPLOADS=3
DOCUMENT_MAX_CONCURRENT_DOWNLOADS=6

# Sign-in (ADR 0034). How many reverse proxies sit in front of this copy: exactly that many, each appending the address it received the
# request from to X-Forwarded-For. Set it ONLY behind such a proxy. Unset: the source address of a request is recorded as unknown, and
# addresses are then neither shown nor throttled (the per-account lockout still holds). With no proxy in front, setting it is wrong:
# Next keeps an X-Forwarded-For the client sent, so the "address" would be whatever the client wrote. That lets a client dodge the
# address limit and choose the address shown as the previous sign-in, so it is for local trials only, never a deployment.
# Deployment configuration, not customer policy.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1
#
# The hosts a server action may be called from besides the host it was sent to (Next's own Origin-against-Host check, threat model F9). Behind a reverse
# proxy that rewrites the host, name the public host here (comma-separated EXACT hosts: a pattern such as `*.example.org` is refused and the server does not start,
# since a sibling subdomain is the same site), or every form is refused. The product's own sign-in guard reads this list too, so TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS is not needed
# for it. Unset: only the request's own host, right for development and for a copy with no proxy. Deployment configuration, not customer policy. Read when the server starts.
# SERVER_ACTION_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=perolehan.example.org
#
# Password hashing runs on libuv's thread pool (default 4 threads), which file reads and compression share. So at most one fewer than the
# pool, up to 4, hashes run at once: 3 by default. To allow 4, set UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE=8 in the process environment before it starts
# (it cannot be set from application code). Waiting sign-ins are capped at 64 (HASH_QUEUE_LIMIT); beyond that they are refused at once.
# UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE=8
#
# The session cookie is __Host-perolehan-staff: Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax. Browsers accept a Secure cookie on http://localhost
# but not on http://127.0.0.1 or a LAN address: open the development copy at http://localhost:3000. A real deployment needs HTTPS.
#
# Production only: the key Next encrypts server-action references with. Unset, each build makes its own, so every open form breaks at a
# redeploy and copies behind a load balancer disagree. A base64 AES key (32 random bytes), the same on every copy, kept as a secret.
# NEXT_SERVER_ACTIONS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Demonstration sign-in (npm run demo:accounts): the one password every demonstration account signs in with, so that someone who has loaded the demo can sign in as an
# administrator or as one of its people. For a development or demonstration database ONLY: it is a shared password, written here in the open on purpose, and no deployment
# should ever have it. Copy it into your own .env (or choose your own, at least as long as the organisation's shortest password).
# DEMO_ACCOUNT_PASSWORD=UwGjewiAuF5dzP55Y8xA

# Optional, local only: a scratch database for the checks that need one (`npm run test:db`, scripts/check-config-lock-order.ts). It must
# name a different database from DATABASE_URL; they refuse otherwise. Never the development database. The database tests make a role of
# their own (perolehan_app_test, no login) to stand in for the app's login; it never touches perolehan_app.
# TEST_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://perolehan:perolehan@localhost:5432/perolehan_native_check?schema=public"

# SR-10 payment simulator (review SEC-1). Deployment configuration, not customer policy. Absent by default. The simulator moves no money, yet a simulated "paid" purchase releases the
# documents and makes a sale. Set this to true ONLY on a demonstration or development copy that is not selling for real. Where it is absent: the organisation's simulator switch cannot be
# turned on, a buyer cannot pay on the simulator, and a purchase already paid on it stops releasing the documents (it is listed for the officer as "Simulated, no money received").
# Read when a request is made; restart the server after changing it so every process agrees.
# PAYMENT_SIMULATOR_ALLOWED=true

# Online sealed bids (SR-13, ADR 0036). Deployment configuration, not customer policy: every one is absent by default. Read when a request is made; restart the
# server after changing one so every process agrees. docs/notes/sealed-bids-deployment.md lists what the host must do for the sealing to hold (core dumps and swap
# off, HTTPS only, no proxy logging request bodies, the scanner on the same host) and how to keep the pepper.
#
# Whether this copy may take online bids that are a DEMONSTRATION ("Demonstration — not for real tenders"). Only "true" or "1" allows it. Set it ONLY on a
# demonstration or development copy. Absent: no demonstration exercise is published, and a published one stops taking bids.
# SEALED_BIDS_DEMONSTRATION_ALLOWED=true
#
# Whether this copy may take LIVE online bids. It gates issuing LIVE on the bid sealing policy and publishing a LIVE exercise; an exercise already published LIVE
# keeps taking bids when it is cleared. LIVE also needs the outside review, the procedure amendment and the organisation's acceptance recorded, a malware scanner,
# the pepper below, and the two attestations. Absent: LIVE cannot be issued.
# SEALED_BIDS_LIVE_ALLOWED=true
#
# How many bid parts one process takes at once (a memory guard: a part is held whole while it is checked, scanned and sealed). Each company may also have only one
# upload in flight. Unset, zero or not a whole number: 3.
# BID_UPLOAD_MAX_CONCURRENT=3
#
# The scrypt pepper mixed into every opening officer's unseal passphrase, so a copy of the database alone does not allow offline guessing. Base64 of at least 32
# random bytes (for example: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"), and the name it is known by (letters, digits, dots,
# underscores and hyphens). Both or neither. LIVE requires it; a demonstration wraps without it. Keep it OUT of the repository and the database, and back it up
# apart from the database: losing it loses every exercise wrapped under it. NEVER rotate or remove it while any opening of an exercise wrapped under it can
# still follow (ruling 20y: until the exercise is cancelled, or its main opening is finalised and every late bid not withdrawn is either not admitted or opened
# at a finalised supplementary opening): those exercises could then never be opened. See docs/notes/sealed-bids-deployment.md.
# SEALING_PEPPER=
# SEALING_PEPPER_ID=pepper-2026-10
